.TH SYSTEMD 1 "2026-09-23" "jimmy-generated" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
systemd \- Linux system and service manager for the entire system
.SH DESCRIPTION
Systemd is a system and service manager for Linux-based operating systems that provides a way to manage services and units through a hierarchical system of dependencies and priorities. It runs as the first process on the system, providing parallelization, socket and D-Bus activation, and transactional dependency-based service control logic. The system includes a logging daemon, utilities for controlling system configuration, and daemons for managing network configuration, time synchronization, and log forwarding. It provides tools like systemctl and systemd-analyze to inspect and verify the system state, and allows users to create custom service files in various locations on the system. Systemd also offers a way to edit and modify existing service files through mechanisms like drop-in files and replacement units, providing users with flexibility in configuring and controlling system behavior. By providing a comprehensive management system, systemd simplifies system maintenance and troubleshooting, and improves overall system performance and stability.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B systemctl
List and/or manipulate the state of the system.
.TP
.B systemd-analyze
Inspect or verify the work of systemd.
.TP
.B systemctl list-units
List all known units in the user's session or on the system.
.TP
.B systemd-analyze cat-config
Inspect or verify the work of systemd.
.TP
.B systemctl edit
Edit the unit file.
.TP
.B systemctl edit --full
Edit the unit file and all its associated drop-in files.
.TP
.B systemctl edit --drop-in=drop_in_name
Edit the drop-in file for a unit.
.TP
.B systemctl daemon-reload
Reload the systemd daemon.
.TP
.B systemctl revert
Revert any changes made to a unit.
.TP
.B systemctl list-units --type=target
List all known targets.
.TP
.B systemctl isolate
Isolate the current target and all its units.
.TP
.B systemctl set-default
Set the default target to boot into.
.TP
.B systemctl get-default
Get the current default target.
.TP
.B systemd-delta
See which unit files have been overridden or extended and what exactly has been changed.
.TP
.B systemctl cat unit
View the content of a unit file and all associated drop-in snippets.
.TP
.B systemd.service
View the service unit file.
.TP
.B systemd unit
View the unit file with all its associated drop-in files.
.TP
.B systemd unit --drop-in=drop_in_name
View the drop-in file for a unit.
.TP
.B systemd unit --full unit
View the unit file and all its associated drop-in files.
.TP
.B systemd unit --runtime
Create a new unit file with runtime changes.
